<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="ferulate and sinapate biosynthesis" organism="ARA" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5168" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Arabidopsis thaliana col&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Arabidopsis thaliana col. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; The formation of the free hydroxycinnamic acids such as caffeate, ferulate and sinapate has long been thought to derive through a series of hydroxylation and methylation reactions at the phenolic ring of cinnamic acid (|FRAME:PWY-2181|) |CITS: [BAUCHER98]|. However, intensive research directed towards the biosynthesis of phenylpropanoids has led to a significant change of the layout and interconnectivity of this central pathway (|FRAME:PWY-361|) giving rise to the biosynthesis of a vast variety of major secondary metabolites such as flavonoids, lignins, lignans, stilbenes and aurones |CITS: [LEWIS99]| |CITS: [11960740]| |CITS: [14503002]|. The discovery of new enzymes involved in the metabolic sequence of phenyplpropanoids caused the revision of the pathway generating ferulate and sinapate (this pathway) which constitute precursors for e.g. hydroxycinnamic esters (|FRAME:PWY-3301|) the main secondary metabolites found in the mustard family |CITS: [8972602]|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The main stream in the phenylpropanoid pathway has been demonstrated to lead from p-coumarate to the various monolignols via coumaroyl-CoA and coniferyl aldehyde as the main branching intermediates (|FRAME:PWY-361|). The phenylpropanoid 3-hydroxylation resulting in the formation of 3,4-hydroxylated phenylpropanoids and their further metabolization has been shown to involve hydroxycinnamoyl-CoA esters as substrates |CITS: [11429408]| |CITS: [11967091]| |CITS: [12381722]| |CITS: [15161961]| whereas 5-hydroxylation and 5-methylation occur on the level of hydroxycinnamaldehydes and/or hydroxycinnamyl alcohols |CITS: [10468559]| |CITS: [10430877]|. The formation of the free phenylpropanoid acids ferulate and sinapate is catalyzed via the oxidation of the corresponding cinnamaldehydes as proposed earlier |CITS: [10468559]|. The gene (REF1) encoding the enzyme catalyzing the reaction, i.e. cinnamaldehyde dehydrogenase was characterized in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [14729911]| and shown to possess both coniferyl aldehyde- and sinapyl aldehyde dehydrogenase activity. This enzymatic activity has been found earlier in bacteria where coniferyl aldehyde was converted to ferulate |CITS: [9721273]|.</Description>
  <Comment source="Automated Conversion">This GPML file was automatically generated from PlantCyc flatfiles.</Comment>
